From abb843227fa46bdc3112dff1d9d30756bd25f245 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 05:40:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer, windowing/gbm: HDR10 mapped scene by scene for
 projectors and SDR output, off by default

An expert setting (System > Display, level 3, off by default),
"HDR10 mapped scene by scene (measured)": where Hades maps an HDR10
video itself (to SDR, or with "HDR10 and HDR10+ adapted to the
display"), each picture is mapped by the brightest and the average of
its own scene instead of the static metadata of the film
(dvbridge_plain::scene).

The scenes are measured as for HDR10 sent as Dolby Vision: each P010
picture of VAAPI queued for presentation is measured on the GPU ahead
of it (CRendererVAAPIGLES::MeasureAhead(), dvbridge_measure.c), with a
measurement of its own, and dvbridge_measured.c finds the cuts and the
level 1 of each scene, its average without dovi_tool's floor (a floor
that is for the metadata of a stream, not for a mapping). As each
picture is presented, dvbridge_scene_map decides what it is mapped by,
with the band and the rate that keep it from pumping within a scene. A
seek starts it again. HDR10+ videos keep their own statistics, and a
picture not measured yet is mapped by the static metadata, as before.

Measured on the software bridge with two UHD Blu-ray samples (2200
and 2108 pictures, decoded and measured two pictures ahead, as Kodi does),
mapped to SDR (white 203 cd/m2) and to a projector of 150 cd/m2:
- The second sample (2108 pictures, 41 scenes, all measured in time): the
  99th percentile of the luminance, mean over the scenes, 121.0
  against 111.4 cd/m2 on the projector and 148.1 against 138.3 to SDR
  in the darker half of the scenes (136.4 against 129.4, and 178.6
  against 168.1, in the brighter half); nearer to the source in 29
  (projector) and 30 (SDR) of 36 scenes, the same in 3 and 2, and at
  most 2.2 and 2.9 cd/m2 further in the other 4; the average level 3
  to 6 % higher, nearer to the source's as well.
- The first sample (scenes measured at up to 1000 cd/m2, its master's
  peak): within 2 % of the static mapping, its highlights nearer to
  the source where a scene stays below the film's peak (63.3 against
  53.9 cd/m2 on the projector, 65.9 in the source).
- In both, the level of the output moves within a scene no more than
  with the static mapping (median spread over the source: 0.07 against
  0.09 stops to SDR and 0.11 against 0.15 to the projector in
  the second sample; 0.08 against 0.09 and 0.13 against 0.22 in the first).

The log says once a video what it maps by and from what, and every 240
pictures how many were not measured in time and the values in use. With
the setting off (the default), nothing is measured and every picture is
mapped as before, bit for bit.
